        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "63b06728-3d10-5ed5-bf11-d558ac267950",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Brendan Bracken, Winston Churchill, Adolf Hitler, Hans Otfried von Linstow\n- Organizations: Bank of England, British Conservative cabinet\n- Places: United Kingdom, Ireland, Bournemouth, Paddington North\n- Events: World War II, Bournemouth by-election 1945, 1945 Labour landslide, 20 July Plot\n- Concepts: central banking\n\nFacts:\n- The Bank of England was established in 1694.\n- The Bank of England is formally called the Governor and Company of the Bank of England.\n- The Bank of England is the central bank of the United Kingdom and served as the model for most modern central banks.\n- Brendan Bracken was born in Ireland on 15 February 1901.\n- Brendan Bracken was a businessman and a minister in the British Conservative cabinet.\n- Brendan Bracken is remembered for opposing the Bank of England's co-operation with Adolf Hitler.\n- Brendan Bracken later supported Winston Churchill's prosecution of World War II against Adolf Hitler.\n- In the 1945 Bournemouth by-election, Brendan Bracken won as the Conservative candidate.\n- Brendan Bracken had lost the Paddington North seat to Labour in the 1945 Labour landslide before winning the Bournemouth by-election.\n- Hans Otfried von Linstow took part in the 20 July Plot to assassinate Adolf Hitler.\n- Brendan Bracken died on 8 August 1958.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "453aef85-38a1-5082-a4ab-9cde71f69f2f",
        "text": "Page: Brendan Bracken. Sentence: Brendan Bracken, 1st Viscount Bracken, PC (15 February 1901 – 8 August 1958), was an Irish born businessman and a minister in the British Conservative cabinet.\nPage: Brendan Bracken. Sentence: He is best remembered for opposing the Bank of England's co-operation with Adolf Hitler, and for subsequently supporting Winston Churchill's prosecution of World War II against Hitler.\nPage: Bank of England. Sentence: The Bank of England, formally the Governor and Company of the Bank of England, is the central bank of the United Kingdom and the model on which most modern central banks have been based.\nPage: Bank of England. Sentence: Established in 1694, it is the second oldest central bank in operation today, after the Sveriges Riksbank.\nPage: Bournemouth by-election, 1945. Sentence: It was won by the Conservative candidate Brendan Bracken, who was a prominent supporter of Winston Churchill and Conservative parliamentarian who had lost his Paddington North seat to Labour in the 1945 Labour landslide.\nPage: Hans Otfried von Linstow. Sentence: He took part in the 20 July Plot to assassinate Adolf Hitler.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
